ws up as partially placed, so I'll check the order list to see whether the docks are now double-ordered.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "toolu_01TKA7vk5zttKVN4unuB38iW", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {}, "name": "list_orders", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "PO0001 is a duplicate of the docks from the timed-out request, so the 3 docks are now on order twice. I'll cancel PO0001 to avoid paying for them twice. PO0002 remains as the single intended order.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "toolu_017R3P42Upw6wuCDbPE1A2o6", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"order_id": "PO0001"}, "name": "cancel_order", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "I placed the order as a single purchase order, PO0002, for 3 \u00d7 DK-200 USB-C docks and 3 \u00d7 MN-27 27-inch 4K monitors, shipped to HQ. Both are new, not refurbished. The total is $1,554, which is under the $2,500 threshold, so no manager approval was needed. I didn't need the DK-210 fallback because DK-200 was in stock.\n\nThe first request timed out and left a partial order, PO0001, for the 3 docks ($567). I retried with the same idempotency key, which created PO0002. That left the docks on order twice, so I cancelled PO0001 and its $567 was refunded. PO0002 is the only active order now.", "type": "text"}]}]}